---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';
---

<GuideLayout
  slug="keyboard-shortcuts"
  description="Krova's keyboard shortcuts. On Windows, global shortcuts move the focused window to the previous or next zone, to zone 1 to 9, or to the next monitor (default Ctrl+Alt+Shift). Plus the layout editor's keys. No keyboard shortcuts on macOS yet."
  lead="On Windows, global shortcuts move the window you are working in from zone to zone. Inside the layout editor there are a few more keys. macOS has no global shortcuts yet."
>
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>Windows only for now.</strong> The global shortcuts need Windows. On macOS the Settings window says
      &ldquo;Keyboard shortcuts are not available on this system yet&rdquo;, and you snap by dragging.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="global">Move the focused window (Windows)</h2>
  <p>
    Each shortcut is the <strong>modifier keys</strong> you chose, held together with one more key. By default the
    modifier keys are <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>. They act on the window that has the focus.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Global keyboard shortcuts">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Keys (with the default modifier)</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Left</kbd></th>
          <td>Move the focused window to the previous zone. From the first zone it wraps to the last.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Right</kbd></th>
          <td>Move it to the next zone. From the last zone it wraps to the first.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>1</kbd> to <kbd>9</kbd></th>
          <td>Move it to that zone number on the monitor it is on.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Up</kbd></th>
          <td>
            Send it to the next monitor, to the right and wrapping round, in the zone with the same number. If that monitor&rsquo;s
            layout has fewer zones, it goes to the last one.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>How zones are numbered</h3>
  <p>
    Zones are counted in reading order: top to bottom, and left to right within a row (rows are compared to the nearest 1%).
    In a 2x2 grid, 1 is top left, 2 top right, 3 bottom left and 4 bottom right. In Halves and Thirds, 1 is the leftmost zone.
    The number is the zone&rsquo;s place in that order, not an id you can see. With more than nine zones, <kbd>1</kbd> to
    <kbd>9</kbd> reach the first nine and <kbd>Left</kbd> and <kbd>Right</kbd> reach the others. A number past the last zone
    does nothing.
  </p>

  <h3>Which windows they move</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>The window with the focus. A maximized window is restored first, then moved into the zone.</li>
    <li>
      They do nothing for the desktop, the taskbar, minimized windows, windows of programs running as administrator, or
      Krova&rsquo;s own windows.
    </li>
    <li>They follow the same settings as dragging: animated or instant moves, and the space between zones.</li>
    <li>When <strong>Enabled</strong> is off in the tray menu, the shortcuts are released and do nothing.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Choose other modifier keys</h3>
  <p>
    Open <strong>Settings</strong> from the tray menu, then <strong>Keyboard shortcuts</strong>. <strong>Use keyboard
    shortcuts</strong> switches the feature on or off (on by default). <strong>Modifier keys</strong> offers:
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Modifier key choices">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Choice</th><th scope="col">Note</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Alt</kbd> + <kbd>Shift</kbd></th><td>The default. Accepted for all twelve shortcuts on the Windows 11 PC we tested.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Alt</kbd></th><td>Accepted for all twelve there. Some programming tools use Ctrl+Alt+Left and Right for back and forward.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>Win</kbd> + <kbd>Alt</kbd></th><td>Windows refused all twelve on that PC; it keeps these for itself.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>Win</kbd> + <kbd>Ctrl</kbd></th><td>Windows refused eleven of the twelve on that PC; only Win+Ctrl+Up was accepted.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    That is one PC and one Windows version, so treat it as a hint. Some combinations can be taken by Windows or by other
    apps. When Krova cannot register a shortcut, Settings lists it, for example &ldquo;Win+Alt+3 is already used by another
    app&rdquo;. Pick other modifier keys, or close the app that holds the combination. The list updates as soon as you
    change the choice. If a shortcut does nothing, see <a href={url('/support/#shortcut')}>Troubleshooting</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="editor">In the layout editor</h2>
  <p>
    These work while the editor window has focus. On a Mac, <kbd>Cmd</kbd> takes the place of <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>. The
    single-letter keys are ignored while a text box has focus, so you can still type a layout name.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Editor keyboard shortcuts">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Key</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>Esc</kbd></th><td>Back to the Select tool; if you are already there, clear the selected zone</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>V</kbd></th><td>Split vertically tool: the divider is a vertical line, so zones end up side by side</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>H</kbd></th><td>Split horizontally tool: zones end up stacked</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>Delete</kbd></th><td>Delete the selected zone; a neighbour that shares a full edge takes its space</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd></th><td>Undo the last edit to the current layout</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd></th><td>Save the layout</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>On a focused divider</h3>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Divider keyboard shortcuts">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Key</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Arrow keys</th><td>Move the divider by 1%</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>Shift</kbd> + arrow keys</th><td>Move the divider by 5%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>On a zone</h3>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Zone keyboard shortcuts">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Key</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>Enter</kbd> or <kbd>Space</kbd></th><td>Select the zone (Select tool)</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Arrow keys</th><td>Move the split guide (Split tool); hold <kbd>Shift</kbd> for 5% steps</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>Enter</kbd></th><td>Split the zone at the guide (Split tool)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Tabs</h3>
  <p>
    On the monitor tabs, <kbd>Left</kbd>, <kbd>Right</kbd>, <kbd>Home</kbd> and <kbd>End</kbd> move between monitors. On
    the Editor / Settings switch, <kbd>Left</kbd> and <kbd>Right</kbd> move between the two views.
  </p>

  <h2 id="activation">The activation key is a separate setting</h2>
  <p>
    The key you hold while <em>dragging</em> (Shift by default) is not one of these shortcuts. It only shows the zones
    during a drag. Change it in Settings, Snapping, Activation modifier: Shift, Ctrl, Alt, or the Windows key (Command on a
    Mac). See <a href={url('/guides/first-snap/')}>Your first snap</a>.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
